lesson "What a Vector Is" {
  course: "pure-maths"
  unit: "Vectors"
  difficulty: "Beginner"
  icon: "Move"
  summary: "Some quantities need a direction to mean anything. Magnitude, direction, and why a vector does not care where you draw it."
  skills: ["vec-definition", "vec-magnitude", "vec-scalar-vs-vector"]

  slide "Some Numbers Need a Direction" {
    id: "v1-hook"
    cat: "Two Kinds of Quantity"
    > "I walked $5$ km" tells you how tired I am. "I walked $5$ km **north**" tells
    > you where I ended up. The second is strictly more information, and some
    > questions cannot be answered without it.
    >
    > A **scalar** is a size on its own: mass, time, temperature, speed. A **vector**
    > carries a size *and* a direction: displacement, velocity, force,
    > acceleration.
    >
    > You have been using vectors all through mechanics without the label. Every
    > force arrow was one.
    >
    > Below, every arrow is a $5$ km walk from the same front door. The **size** is
    > identical in all of them and the dashed circle is every place you could possibly
    > end up. Swing the direction round: the number $5$ never changes, and where you
    > finish changes completely. That difference is the entire lesson.

    scene plane {
      x: [-7, 7]
      y: [-6, 6]
      aspect: equal
      axes: false
      param th = 40 { range: [0, 360], step: 5 }
      circle reach = (0, 0) { r: 5, color: neutral, style: dashed, opacity: 0 }
      vector walk = (0, 0) -> (5*cos(rad(th)), 5*sin(rad(th))) { color: primary, width: 5 }
      point home = (0, 0) { color: neutral, r: 8 }
      label at (-1.1, -0.9) = "front door" { color: neutral }
      point end = (5*cos(rad(th)), 5*sin(rad(th))) { color: accent, r: 9 }
      label at (-6.9, 5.4) = "distance walked: 5 km, always" { color: neutral }
      label at (-6.9, 4.6) = "finishing at ${5*cos(rad(th))} east, ${5*sin(rad(th))} north" { color: accent }
      slider th { label: "which way did you set off?" }
    }

    sort {
      ask "Sort each quantity by whether it needs a direction to be fully described."
      skill: "vec-scalar-vs-vector"
      bin "Scalar": ["mass, 5 kg", "time, 20 s", "speed, 30 m/s", "temperature, 18°C"]
      bin "Vector": ["velocity, 30 m/s east", "force, 12 N downwards", "displacement, 5 km north"]
      hint "Ask whether the phrase would be incomplete without a direction attached."
      ! "Speed and velocity are the pair worth watching. Speed is $30$ m/s; velocity is $30$ m/s *in a stated direction*. A car going round a roundabout at a steady speed has a constantly changing velocity, which is exactly why it needs a force."
    }
  }

  slide "Magnitude and Direction" {
    id: "v1-magnitude"
    cat: "Two Kinds of Quantity"
    > Draw a vector as an arrow: its **length** is the magnitude and the way it
    > **points** is the direction. Written $\mathbf{a}$ in bold, or
    > $\overrightarrow{AB}$ when named by its endpoints.
    >
    > The magnitude, written $|\mathbf{a}|$, is Pythagoras once again:
    >
    > $$|\mathbf{a}| = \sqrt{a_1^2 + a_2^2}$$
    >
    > Exactly the same formula as the distance between two points and as the modulus
    > of a complex number. Three topics, one triangle.
    >
    > Drag the arrowhead and watch the length and the angle.

    scene plane {
      x: [-6, 6]
      y: [-5, 5]
      aspect: equal
      grid
      axes
      param ax = 4 { range: [-5, 5], step: 1 }
      param ay = 3 { range: [-4, 4], step: 1 }
      vector a = (0, 0) -> (ax, ay) { color: primary, width: 4 }
      line across = (0, 0) -> (ax, 0) { color: warning, width: 3, style: dashed }
      line up = (ax, 0) -> (ax, ay) { color: success, width: 3, style: dashed }
      point tip = (ax, ay) { drag: xy -> (ax, ay), color: accent, r: 9, snap: 1 }
      arc ang = (0, 0) { r: 1.1, from: 0, to: mod(deg(atan2(ay, ax)), 360), color: neutral, width: 2 }
      label at (-5.7, 4.5) = "components ${ax} across, ${ay} up" { color: neutral }
      label at (-5.7, 3.6) = "magnitude = ${hypot(ax, ay)}" { color: primary }
      label at (-5.7, 2.7) = "direction = ${mod(deg(atan2(ay, ax)), 360)}°" { color: accent }
    }

    goal "Build a vector of magnitude exactly 5" {
      when: abs(hypot(ax, ay) - 5) < 0.01
      hint: "3 and 4 in some order, or 5 and 0."
    }
  }

  slide "Position Does Not Matter" {
    id: "v1-free"
    cat: "Free Vectors"
    > This is the idea that separates vectors from points, and it takes a moment to
    > accept.
    >
    > **A vector has no location.** It is a size and a direction, and that is all. Two
    > arrows of the same length pointing the same way are the *same vector*, however
    > far apart you draw them.
    >
    > "Three metres east" is three metres east whether you start from your front door
    > or from the far side of the country. The instruction is identical; only the
    > outcome differs.
    >
    > All three arrows below are the same vector. This is exactly why tip-to-tail
    > addition is allowed: sliding an arrow somewhere else does not change it.

    scene plane {
      x: [-6, 6]
      y: [-4, 4]
      aspect: equal
      grid
      axes
      vector v1 = (-5, -3) -> (-2, -1) { color: primary, width: 4 }
      vector v2 = (-1, 1) -> (2, 3) { color: primary, width: 4 }
      vector v3 = (2, -3) -> (5, -1) { color: primary, width: 4 }
      label at (0, 3.6) = "same length, same direction, so the same vector" { anchor: middle, color: primary }
    }

    quiz {
      ask "Two arrows are drawn in different places on a grid. Both are $4$ units long and both point north-east. Are they the same vector?"
      skill: "vec-definition"
      * "Yes — a vector is only a magnitude and a direction"
      - "No, they start at different points" { why: "Where you draw it is not part of what a vector is. Only its length and direction count." }
      - "Only if they start at the origin" { why: "Vectors drawn from the origin have a special name, position vectors, and they come later. An ordinary vector is free to be drawn anywhere." }
      hint "Which two pieces of information define a vector?"
      ! "The same vector. Being able to slide an arrow without changing it is what makes vector addition work."
    }
  }

  slide "Compute a Magnitude" {
    id: "v1-compute"
    cat: "Free Vectors"
    numeric {
      ask "A vector has components $7$ across and $24$ up. What is its magnitude?"
      skill: "vec-magnitude"
      answer: 25
      expect: hypot(7, 24)
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "$\sqrt{7^2 + 24^2}$."
      ! "$\sqrt{49 + 576} = \sqrt{625} = 25$. The $7, 24, 25$ triangle, which turns up almost as often as $3,4,5$ and $5,12,13$."
    }
  }

  slide "The Negative of a Vector" {
    id: "v1-negative"
    cat: "Free Vectors"
    > $-\mathbf{a}$ is the same length as $\mathbf{a}$, pointing the **opposite way**.
    > Negating a vector reverses it; it never shrinks it.
    >
    > So $|-\mathbf{a}| = |\mathbf{a}|$, always. A magnitude is a length and lengths
    > are never negative, exactly as with the modulus of a complex number.
    >
    > That also means $\overrightarrow{AB} = -\overrightarrow{BA}$: walking from $A$
    > to $B$ is the exact reverse of walking from $B$ to $A$.

    quiz {
      ask "If $|\mathbf{a}| = 10$, what is $|-3\mathbf{a}|$?"
      skill: "vec-magnitude"
      * "$30$"
      - "$-30$" { why: "A magnitude is a length, so it can never come out negative however many minus signs are involved." }
      - "$10$" { why: "The $3$ does stretch it. Only the minus sign leaves the length alone." }
      hint "The $3$ triples the length. What does the minus sign do to a length?"
      ! "$30$. Multiplying by $-3$ reverses the direction and triples the length, and only the tripling shows up in the magnitude."
    }
  }
}
